\section{Why not a plain join}{


Joining data frames on raw URL strings misses \verb{http://example.com:80/a}
against \verb{http://example.com/a}. Joining them on a \emph{cleaned} string
overmatches instead, because cleaning is a display policy: it can strip a
trailing slash, a query parameter or a \code{www.} that genuinely distinguished
two resources. These joins use \code{\link[=get_url_key]{get_url_key()}}, so what matches is what rurl
considers the same resource -- and no cleaning or display option can change
that.
}

\section{Row order}{


Order is part of the contract, not an artifact of the implementation:

\describe{
\item{\code{url_inner_join}}{matching pairs in \code{x} order, \code{y} matches in \code{y}
order within each \code{x} row.}
\item{\code{url_left_join}}{every \code{x} row in \code{x} order; unmatched \code{x} rows carry
a missing \code{y} payload typed from \code{y}'s own columns.}
\item{\code{url_right_join}}{the exact mirror: every \code{y} row in \code{y} order, \code{x}
matches in \code{x} order.}
\item{\code{url_full_join}}{the left-join result, then the \code{y} rows it never
consumed, in \code{y} order.}
\item{\code{url_semi_join}}{each \code{x} row with at least one match, once, \code{x}
columns only.}
\item{\code{url_anti_join}}{each \code{x} row with no match, once, \code{x} columns only.}
}

Duplicate keys expand as a Cartesian product. Rows are never silently
discarded to "resolve" a duplicate -- multiplicity is a fact you declare with
\code{relationship} or narrow with \code{multiple}.
}

\section{Rows that cannot be keyed}{


A URL the standard cannot parse, or a missing or empty one, has no identity
and never matches -- not even another unparseable URL. \code{invalid} decides what
happens to those rows: \code{"keep"} (default) leaves them in, unmatched, so a
left join still returns them; \code{"drop"} removes them before matching;
\code{"error"} refuses the join and reports the offending row positions.

\code{warnings} is a separate axis for rows that \emph{did} parse but carry a note --
userinfo on a scheme-less input, or a host whose public-suffix annotation did
not resolve. \code{"allow"} (default) matches them normally, \code{"reject"} makes them
ineligible to match without removing them, and \code{"error"} refuses the join.

\code{url_anti_join()} keeps non-keyable \code{x} rows, because a row that cannot match
anything is exactly what an anti join asks for.
}

\section{Conditions}{


Failures raise typed conditions -- \code{rurl_url_join_input_error},
\code{rurl_url_join_policy_error}, \code{rurl_url_join_suffix_error},
\code{rurl_url_join_key_name_error}, \code{rurl_url_join_relationship_error},
\code{rurl_url_join_invalid_error} and \code{rurl_url_join_warning_error}, all
inheriting from \code{rurl_url_join_error} -- so they can be caught precisely.
Messages report row \emph{positions} and truncated keys, never URL content, so a
credential in the input cannot leak into an error message.
}

